          "Keep the roles distinct. The host is the application that coordinates the experience. An MCP client manages a protocol connection. An MCP server advertises capabilities and handles requests for those capabilities. A model may decide that a tool is relevant, but the host remains responsible for deciding whether the proposed operation is allowed. The model should never become the authorization boundary.",
          "MCP is commonly built on JSON-RPC 2.0 message semantics. A request has an identifier and method, a response correlates to that identifier, and notifications do not expect a response. Correct implementations must handle malformed messages, unknown methods, request cancellation where supported, and connection closure without confusing protocol errors with application-level failures."
